{"type": "message", "id": "7ef46950", "parentId": "2dea81ca", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:32:02.029Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:0", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"exoplanets radial velocity method discovered before 2020 eccentricity mass 5 Earth masses orbital period 100 days\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 880,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c1979f33a9cd26b9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nList of exoplanets detected by radial velocity - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c1979f33a9cd26b9\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_exoplanets_detected_by_radial_velocity\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"795058f07aadf7e7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe true masses can be determined when astrometry calculates the inclination of the orbit. There are 160 members of the multi-planet systems –– 21 confirmed and 139 candidates. The most massive confirmed exoplanet is <strong>Iota Draconis b</strong>, which masses 9.40 MJ (i.e.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"795058f07aadf7e7\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 3, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb080a79bf525717\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDetermining the true mass of radial-velocity exoplanets with Gaia - Nine planet candidates in the brown dwarf or stellar regime and 27 confirmed planets | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb080a79bf525717\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2021/01/aa39168-20/aa39168-20.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b9c5a3e7672cc19d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe also confirm the planetary nature of 27 exoplanets, including HD 10180 c, d and g. Studying the orbital periods, eccentricities, and host-star metallicities in the BD domain, we found distributions with respect to true masses consistent with other publications. The distribution of orbital periods shows of a void of BD detections below ~100 d, while eccentricity and metallicity distributions agree with a transition between BDs similar to planets and BDs similar to stars in the range 40–50 MJ.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b9c5a3e7672cc19d\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 21, 2020\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"80e000800753dfa8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDetection and characterisation of a 106-day transiting Jupiter: TOI-2449 b/NGTS-36 b | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"80e000800753dfa8\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/11/aa55168-25/aa55168-25.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9029c8076176c461\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe report the discovery and characterisation of <strong>TOI-2449 b / NGTS-36 b</strong>, a 106-day warm giant exoplanet orbiting a G-type star. This planet was first identified as a single transit event in the TESS data. Ground-based photometric follow-up with NGTS and radial velocity follow-up solved the orbital ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9029c8076176c461\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"November 24, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e89388478041f87f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExoplanets Catalogue Analysis: The Distribution of Exoplanets at FGK Stars by Mass and Orbital Period Accounting for the Observational Selection in the Radial Velocity Method | MDPI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e89388478041f87f\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4433/14/2/353\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0f615692599842a4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIf this fraction of the orbit is small, the Kepler curve degenerates into a linear or quadratic drift of the star’s radial velocity, which indicates the presence of long-periodic bodies in the system, but does not allow their period and mass to be determined. For example, planets with P/T &gt; 2.5 (HD 221420 b, Pr0211 c, HAT-P-17 c, HR 5183 b, HD 190984 b, and HD 133131 B b) are in highly eccentric orbits.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0f615692599842a4\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 10, 2023\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.mdpi.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4ffb2fc25bd246f1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nradial velocity exoplanet: Topics by Science.gov\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4ffb2fc25bd246f1\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.science.gov/topicpages/r/radial+velocity+exoplanet.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cbb079743b31a8b1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAccording to the derived upper limits, KOI-2706.01 has a mass significantly smaller than 12 Jupiter masses, which directly indicates its planetary nature. We show that KOI-2687.01 and KOI-2687.02, which have Earth-size or white dwarf-size radii according to photometric data, cannot be white dwarfs, and are therefore exoplanets. Radial velocity analysis for KOI-974, an F-type star, has shown noticeable variations with a half-amplitude of 400 ms-1, which correlate poorly with the phase of its orbital rotation.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cbb079743b31a8b1\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.science.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dc9ee374370a9240\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRadial Velocity Planet Resources in the Exoplanet Archive\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dc9ee374370a9240\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/docs/rv.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9812744c9b301bef\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe motion is detected using ... on the mass of the planet and its orbital period and eccentricity. Reviews of the method can be found in Radial Velocity by Lovis &amp; Fischer. Planets discovered via the transit method which meet the Archive&#x27;s exoplanet criteria are included ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9812744c9b301bef\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 27, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e499bf2fb36a1caf\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDiscoveries of exoplanets - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e499bf2fb36a1caf\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discoveries_of_exoplanets\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1de1e8c1afca3368\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe planet orbits its parent star at a distance of less than 0.03 AU and has a minimum mass estimated at 1.9 times that of Earth. As of January 2010, this is the lightest known extrasolar planet to orbit a main-sequence star. 30 planets: On October 19, it was announced that 30 new planets were discovered, all were detected by the radial velocity method. It is the most planets ever announced in a single day during the exoplanet era.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1de1e8c1afca3368\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"2 weeks ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"84a33624486f693b\\\">>>\\n