iversit´e de Gen`eve Debra A. Fischer\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3ec059fa9bc6e4bc\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://sites.astro.caltech.edu/~lah/review/Radial_Velocity.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3cca45857acd9492\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n4.— Timeline of exoplanet detections in a mass vs. year of discovery diagram. Symbol sizes are proportional to · orbital eccentricity. The continuously decreasing detection threshold illustrates the progress of radial velocity surveys and · shows that planet searches have now entered the Earth-mass domain.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3cca45857acd9492\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"sites.astro.caltech.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4b4ae78150412795\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe Masses of a Sample of Radial-velocity Exoplanets with Astrometric\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4b4ae78150412795\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.raa-journal.org/issues/all/2023/v23n5/202305/P020240604451687678766.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8386eadfa98bd4b4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>3.27 ± 0.19 au</strong>, an eccentricity of 0.4805 ± 0.06 and an\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8386eadfa98bd4b4\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.raa-journal.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"52cd15ca4464017c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExoplanets Catalogue Analysis: The Distribution of Exoplanets at FGK Stars by Mass and Orbital Period Accounting for the Observational Selection in the Radial Velocity Method | MDPI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"52cd15ca4464017c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4433/14/2/353\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b9e8a8ec47e46e22\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHowever, in most case