 found <strong>exoplanet orbits beyond 0.1 AU to be eccentric, particularly for large planets</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"71610f288f461ecb\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"1 month ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "exoplanet radial velocity eccentricity 0.3 0.4 0.5 discovered before 2020 5 Earth masses 100 days period", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 1074, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"124974b4fd10d386\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nList of exoplanets detected by radial velocity - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"124974b4fd10d386\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_exoplanets_detected_by_radial_velocity", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a783f0356501afce\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe most massive exoplanet candidate is <strong>HD 217786 b</strong>, which masses 12.98 MJ; the least massive confirmed planet is HD 10180 b, which masses 0.004 MJ or 3.1 M🜨. The longest period of any confirmed exoplanet is 47 Ursae Majoris d, which takes 14002 days or 38.33 years to make one trip around ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a783f0356501afce\">>>", "published": "December 3, 2024", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a0c387e017b60053\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDiscoveries of exoplanets - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a0c387e017b60053\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discoveries_of_exoplanets", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ba186ad2dbe55265\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe planet orbits its parent star ... than 0.03 AU and has a minimum mass estimated at 1.9 times that of Earth. As of January 2010, this is the lightest known extrasolar planet to orbit a main-sequence star. 30 planets: On October 19, it was announced that 30 new planets were discovered, all were detected by the radial velocity method. It is the most planets ever announced in a single day during the exoplanet ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ba186ad2dbe55265\">>>", "published": "2 weeks ago", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"34372c29bee26f61\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRV-exoplanet eccentricities: Good, Beta, and Best | Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society | Oxford Academic\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"34372c29bee26f61\">>>", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/539/2/727/8098228", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e6c1bf3f27b6edbb\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe examine the eccentricity distribution(s) of radial velocity (RV) detected exoplanets. Previously, the eccentricity distribution was found to be described well by a Beta distribution with shape parameters |$a=0.867\\,\\mathrm{ and}\\, b=3.03$|⁠. Increasing the sample size by a factor of 2.25, we find that the cumulative distribution function regression method now prefers a mixture model of Rayleigh + Exponential distributions over the Beta distribution, with an increase in Bayesian evidence of |$\\Delta \\ln {Z}\\sim 77$| (⁠|$12.6\\, \\sigma$|⁠).\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e6c1bf3f27b6edbb\">>>", "published": "April 12, 2025", "siteName": "academic.oup.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"02bab705ac4b21b6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nradial velocity exoplanet: Topics by Science.gov\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"02bab705ac4b21b6\">>>", "url": "https://www.science.gov/topicpages/r/radial+velocity+exoplanet", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"62f93f3d49367c70\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nMost of the companion stars were determined to have masses of K and M dwarf stars (0.3-0.8 Mâ), and allowed for an investigation into the mass-radius relationship of M and K dwarfs. Among the cases are KIC 9163796, a 122.2 day period &quot;heartbeat star&quot;, a recently-discovered class of eccentric binaries known for tidal distortions and pulsations, with a high eccentricity (e~0.75) and KIC 11244501, a 0.29 day period, contact binary with a double-lined spectrum and mass ratio (q~0.45).\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"62f93f3d49367c70\">>>", "siteName": "www.science.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a8be8e5aab45c4b6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDetection and characterisation of a 106-day transiting Jupiter: TOI-2449 b/NGTS-36 b | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a8be8e5aab45c4b6\">>>", "url": "https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/11/aa55168-25/aa55168-25.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e0b46e2474683163\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe report the discovery and characterisation of <strong>TOI-2449 b / NGTS-36 b</strong>, a 106-day warm giant exoplanet orbiting a G-type star. This planet was first identified as a single transit event in the TESS data. Ground-based photometric follow-up with NGTS and radial velocity follow-up solved the orbital ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e0b46e2474683163\">>>", "published": "November 24, 2025", "siteName": "www.aanda.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3ec059fa9bc6e4bc\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRadial Velocity Christophe Lovis Universit´e de Gen`eve Debra A. Fischer\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3ec059fa9bc6e4bc\">>>", "url": "https://sites.astro.caltech.edu/~lah/review/Radial_Velocity.pdf", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3cca45857acd9492\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n4.— Timeline of exoplanet detections in a mass vs. year of discovery diagram. Symbol sizes are proportional to · orbital eccentricity. The continuously decreasing detection threshold illustrates the progress of radial velocity surveys and · shows that planet searches have now entered the Earth-mass domain.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3cca45857acd9492\">>>", "siteName": "sites.astro.caltech.edu"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4b4ae78150412795\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe Masses of a Sample of Radial-velocity Exoplanets with Astrometric\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4b4ae78150412795\">>>", "url": "https://www.raa-journal.org/issues/all/2023/v23n5/202305/P020240604451687678766.pdf", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8386eadfa98bd4b4\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n<strong>3.27 ± 0.19 au</strong>, an eccentricity of 0.4805 ± 0.06 and an\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8386eadfa98bd4b4\">>>", "siteName": "www.raa-journal.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"52cd15ca4464017c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nExoplanets Catalogue Analysis: The Distribution of Exoplanets at FGK Stars by Mass and Orbital Period Accounting for the Observational Selection in the Radial Velocity Method | MDPI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"52cd15ca4464017c\">>>", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4433/14/2/353", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b9e8a8ec47e46e22\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHowever, in most cases the number of radial velocity measurements leading to the discovery of a low mass planet (&lt;0.14 Jupiter masses) is in the range 100–400, which corresponds to the value of <strong>γ = 0.5…1.0, on average 0.75</strong>, which is accepted ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b9e8a8ec47e46e22\">>>", "published": "February 10, 2023", "siteName": "www.mdpi.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb363982d423fec5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRadial velocity - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb363982d423fec5\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radial_velocity", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4d2525ffd35c435a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIt has been suggested that planets with high eccentricities calculated by this method may in fact be two-planet systems of circular or near-circular resonant orbit. The radial velocity method to detect exoplanets is based on the detection of variations in the velocity of the central star, due to the changing direction of the gravitational pull from an (unseen) exoplanet as it orbits the star.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"4d2525ffd35c435a\">>>", "published": "1 week ago", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dbf3e531f2d3a863\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nExoplanet orbital and physical parameters - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"dbf3e531f2d3a863\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exoplanet_orbital_and_physical_parameters", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"71610f288f461ecb\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWith the exoplanet sample known ... comparable to Earth could be hidden in known orbital solutions of eccentric super-Earths and Neptune mass planets&quot;. Radial velocity surveys found <strong>exoplanet orbits beyond 0.1 AU to be eccentric, particularly for large planets</strong>....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"71610f288f461ecb\">>>", "published": "1 month ago", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775298786105}}
