{"type": "message", "id": "dc6b132f", "parentId": "d61150ed", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:34:29.130Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I found that HD 20794 d was discovered in 2011 using radial velocity at La Silla Observatory in Chile. However, the original discovery in 2011 had a period of about 90 days, not the outer planet with 647.6 days that has eccentricity 0.45.\n\nLet me search for more exoplanets with high eccentricity that meet the criteria."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "functions.web_search:21", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"query": "exoplanets radial velocity discovered before 2020 eccentricity 0.3 0.4 0.5 mass 5 Earth", "count": 10}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "kimi-k2.5", "usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 0, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775298853767, "responseId": "chatcmpl-4ed92093-36eb-9749-a94c-9657398af033"}}
{"type": "message", "id": "21553cb2", "parentId": "dc6b132f", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:34:30.195Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:21", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"exoplanets radial velocity discovered before 2020 eccentricity 0.3 0.4 0.5 mass 5 Earth\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1060,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"54362f7af44d66f1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nList of exoplanets detected by radial velocity - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"54362f7af44d66f1\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_exoplanets_detected_by_radial_velocity\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"42ae0c498a8e8fbb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe following is a list of 456 extrasolar planets that were only detected by radial velocity method –– <strong>31 confirmed and 323 candidates</strong>, sorted by orbital periods. Since none of these planets are transiting or directly observed, they do not have measured radii and generally their masses ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"42ae0c498a8e8fbb\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 3, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7d6372405e4796bd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nInstituto de Astrofísica e Ciências do Espaço\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7d6372405e4796bd\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.iastro.pt/research/paperDetails.html?ID=3040\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cea631bb940af4d4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFurther analysis of these RVs reveals the presence of an additional planet, <strong>HD 35843 b</strong>, with a period of 9.90 days and a minimum mass of 5.84 ± 0.84 M⊕. For HD 35843 c, a joint photometric and spectroscopic analysis yields a radius of 2.54 ± 0.08 R⊕, a mass of 11.32 ± 1.60 M⊕, and ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cea631bb940af4d4\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.iastro.pt\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7dd2ee3a1ef3f74e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDiscoveries of exoplanets - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7dd2ee3a1ef3f74e\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discoveries_of_exoplanets\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e568654e7a5c2ff\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe planet orbits its parent star ... than 0.03 AU and has a minimum mass estimated at 1.9 times that of Earth. As of January 2010, this is the lightest known extrasolar planet to orbit a main-sequence star. 30 planets: On October 19, it was announced that 30 new planets were discovered, all were detected by the radial velocity method. It is the most planets ever announced in a single day during the exoplanet ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e568654e7a5c2ff\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"2 weeks ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8ce2250d15410dc1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGiant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT ‘EM) Survey. II. Discovery of a Failed Hot Jupiter on a 2.7 Yr, Highly Eccentric Orbit* | Request PDF\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8ce2250d15410dc1\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354732066_Giant_Outer_Transiting_Exoplanet_Mass_GOT_'EM_Survey_II_Discovery_of_a_Failed_Hot_Jupiter_on_a_27_Yr_Highly_Eccentric_Orbit\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"247a31f1773b20e2\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThis supports the hypothesis that dynamical interactions with wide stellar companions plays an important role in the formation of highly eccentric exoplanets. ... Paul A. Dalba · Heather A. Knutson ... The observed correlation between outer giant planets and inner super-Earths is emerging as an important constraint on planet formation theories. In this study we focus on Kepler-167, which is currently the only system known to contain both inner transiting super-Earths and a confirmed outer transiting gas giant companion beyond 1 au. Using long term radial velocity monitoring, we measure the mass of the gas giant Kepler-167e (P = 1071 days) to be 1.01^(+0.16)_(−0.15) M_J, thus confirming it as a Jupiter analog.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"247a31f1773b20e2\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"October 1, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f18477c074b11662\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRadial Velocity Christophe Lovis Universit´e de Gen`eve Debra A. Fischer\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f18477c074b11662\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://sites.astro.caltech.edu/~lah/review/Radial_Velocity.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ec07f681ece5e06f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n4.— Timeline of exoplanet detections in a mass vs. year of discovery diagram. Symbol sizes are proportional to · orbital eccentricity. The continuously decreasing detection threshold illustrates the progress of radial velocity surveys and · shows that planet searches have now entered the Earth-mass domain.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ec07f681ece5e06f\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"sites.astro.caltech.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4227374a15b6b77c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRV-exoplanet eccentricities: Good, Beta, and Best | Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society | Oxford Academic\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4227374a15b6b77c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/539/2/727/8098228\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2237e7f52e3110e0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nABSTRACT. We examine the eccentricity distribution(s) of radial velocity (RV) detected exoplanets. Previously, the eccentricity distribution was found to b\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2237e7f52e3110e0\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 12, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"academic.oup.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"36a96f0cbd7a6b6c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nradial velocity exoplanet: Topics by Science.gov\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"36a96f0cbd7a6b6c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.science.gov/topicpages/r/radial+velocity+exoplanet\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"62ef3cb42336f709\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe synergy between Kepler and the ground-based radial velocity (RV) surveys have made numerous discoveries of small and rocky exoplanets, opening the age of Earth analogs. However, most (29/33) of the RV-detected exoplanets that are smaller than 3 Earth radii do not have their masses constrained to better than 20% - limited by the current RV precision (1-2 m/s).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"62ef3cb42336f709\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.science.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"73c776fd9ffe4046\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDetection and characterisation of a 106-day transiting Jupiter: TOI-2449 b/NGTS-36 b | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"73c776fd9ffe4046\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/11/aa55168-25/aa55168-25.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<