vered in 2011 using the radial velocity technique, which <strong>detects the minute gravitational influence of the planet on its host star</strong>. This method measures variations in the velocity of the star caused by the ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"37a4e5997da14a36\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 31, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"bytebridge.medium.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"44d43032c032d278\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d - NASA Science\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"44d43032c032d278\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanet-catalog/hd-20794-d/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9c93c5c3cc616132\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d is a super Earth exoplanet that orbits a G-type star. Its mass is 4.8 Earths, it takes 90.3 days to complete one orbit of its star, and is <strong>0.3499 AU</strong> from its star. Its discovery was announced in 2011.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9c93c5c3cc616132\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 13, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"science.nasa.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f74df1158f9bbef0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRevisiting the multi-planetary system of the nearby star HD 20794 - Confirmation of a low-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby G-dwarf | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f74df1158f9bbef0\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/01/aa51769-24/aa51769-24.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a4aa175767395f09\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n(2011) reports the discovery of ... ± 0.053 d and an amplitude of 0.56 ± 0.10 m s−1; and HD 20794 d with a period of <strong>90.31 ± 0.18 d and an amplitude of 0.85 ± 0.10 m s−1</strong>. Feng et al....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a4aa175767395f09\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 28, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3c2614408854ad13\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nNewly discovered super-Earth orbits in and out of its star's habitable zone. Could life survive its extreme climate? | Space\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3c2614408854ad13\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.space.com/the-universe/exoplanets/newly-discovered-super-earth-orbits-in-and-out-of-its-stars-habitable-zone-could-life-survive-its-extreme-climate\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7bbba598036ce519\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>Earth and was found by astronomers using the ESPRESSO and HARPS spectrographs on the European Southern Observatory&#x27;s telescopes in Chile</strong>. These instruments measure what&#x27;s termed &#x27;radial velocity&#x27; — the amount by which a star wobbles around ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7bbba598036ce519\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 28, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.space.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3abfefdbdba0a215\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEvidence for at least three planet candidates orbiting HD 20794 | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3abfefdbdba0a215\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2017/09/aa30406-17/aa30406-17.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"17e3a26f22c7e1ce\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe diagnose the noise using the differences between radial velocities measured at different wavelength ranges, so-called “differential radial velocities”. Results. We apply this method to the radial velocities measured by HARPS, and <strong>identify four signals at 18, 89, 147 and 330 d</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"17e3a26f22c7e1ce\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"September 18, 2017\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"21bd2f7c7a71c1ff\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExoplanet HD 20794 d in the habitable zone | BBC Sky at Night Magazine\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"21bd2f7c7a71c1ff\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.skyatnightmagazine.com/news/hd-20794-d\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c0a058582c97c29f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>Dr Michael Cretignier from the University of Oxford in the UK first discovered the exoplanet candidate that would later be confirmed as HD 20794 d</strong>. He was analysing archive data recorded by the HARPS (High Accuracy Radial Velocity Planet Searcher) ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c0a058582c97c29f\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 29, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.skyatnightmagazine.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2d84c897a485f109\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA&A 534, A58 (2011) DOI: 10.1051/0004-6361/201117055 c⃝ESO 2011 Astronomy &\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2d84c897a485f109\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/pdf/2011/10/aa17055-11.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b16ab95ff368695f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nF. Pepe et al.: The HARPS search for Earth-like planets in the habitable zone. I. Fig. 22. Top: residuals of the radial velocities of HD 20794 to the three-\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b16ab95ff368695f\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d43a243a2a250f9a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n82 G. Eridani - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d43a243a2a250f9a\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82_G._Eridani\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c7b0eef84ed4aa78\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nData for the research was gathered at the High Accuracy Radial Velocity Planet Searcher spectrograph at La Silla Observatory in Chile. With the discovery, HD 20794 d became the <strong>12th-closest potentially habitable planet to Earth</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c7b0eef84ed4aa78\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 12, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8dcca8cde04626ef\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRevisiting the Multi-planetary System of the Nearby star HD 20794: Confirmation of a Low-mass Planet in the Habitable Zone of a Nearby G-dwarf - Astrobiology\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8dcca8cde04626ef\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://astrobiology.com/2025/02/revisiting-the-multi-planetary-system-of-the-nearby-star-hd-20794-confirmation-of-a-low-mass-planet-in-the-habitable-zone-of-a-nearby-g-dwarf.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cfcd55f623ee145d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBright and close-by G-to-M type stars are preferential targets in radial velocity surveys to find Earth analogs. We present an analysis of the RV data of the star HD 20794, a target whose planetary system has been extensively debated in the literature. The broad time span of the observations makes it possible to find planets with signal semi-amplitudes below 1 m/s in the habitable zone. We monitored the system with ESPRESSO. We joined ESPRESSO data with the HARPS data, including archival data and new measurements from a recent program.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cfcd55f623ee145d\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 4, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"astrobiology.com\"\n    }\n  ]\