", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cfcd55f623ee145d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nBright and close-by G-to-M type stars are preferential targets in radial velocity surveys to find Earth analogs. We present an analysis of the RV data of the star HD 20794, a target whose planetary system has been extensively debated in the literature. The broad time span of the observations makes it possible to find planets with signal semi-amplitudes below 1 m/s in the habitable zone. We monitored the system with ESPRESSO. We joined ESPRESSO data with the HARPS data, including archival data and new measurements from a recent program.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cfcd55f623ee145d\">>>", "published": "February 4, 2025", "siteName": "astrobiology.com"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775298980484}}
{"type": "message", "id": "3ed1c577", "parentId": "49a8aea6", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:36:39.239Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Based on my research, I found that HD 20794 d was discovered in 2011 using the radial velocity method at La Silla Observatory in Chile. The planet has a mass of about 4.8 Earth masses and an orbital period of about 90 days.\n\nHowever, I need to find the exoplanet with the largest eccentricity. Let me search for more exoplanets with high eccentricity discovered by radial velocity before 2020."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "functions.web_search:24", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"query": "exoplanet \"eccentricity\" 0.4 0.5 0.6 radial velocity discovered 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019", "count": 10}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "kimi-k2.5", "usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 0, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775298980487, "responseId": "chatcmpl-b38d2e11-9eae-908e-8dc3-6f4505377117"}}
{"type": "message", "id": "cc34bd01", "parentId": "3ed1c577", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:36:40.568Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:24", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"exoplanet \\\"eccentricity\\\" 0.4 0.5 0.6 radial velocity discovered 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1325,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d1c4891255592128\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEvidence for a Peak at ∼0.3 in the Eccentricity Distribution of Typical Super-Jovian Exoplanets\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d1c4891255592128\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.18877\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"989444477c0943d5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOver the past 30 years of exoplanet science, this fact has enabled us to shed light on how planets form and evolve. <strong>The overall eccentricity distribution of exoplanets discovered with the radial velocity (RV) method is consistent with a beta distribution peaking at e=0</strong> (Kipping, 2013).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"989444477c0943d5\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 23, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3ebb131b1de03931\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRV-exoplanet eccentricities: Good, Beta, and Best | Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society | Oxford Academic\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3ebb131b1de03931\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/539/2/727/8098228\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"aa658921e8333834\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn this paper, we investigate how the RV discoveries of the past |$\\\\sim 11$| yr affect the eccentricity distribution of RV-discovered exoplanets. Whilst most exoplanets have been discovered with the transit method (primarily from Kepler and TESS, Borucki et al. 2010; Ricker et al. 2015), the number of RV planets has still more than doubled since K13.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"aa658921e8333834\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 12, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"academic.oup.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"98d0540f18815dc9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nPlanet–planet scattering as the source of the highest eccentricity exoplanets | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"98d0540f18815dc9\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2019/09/aa35744-19/aa35744-19.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0017291aa83cbba8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSince our investigation is focused on the highest eccentricities, we are effectively taking the radial velocity sample with the most reliable eccentricity estimates. Previous works have shown that strong dynamical instabilities that produce very eccentric giant planets will typically destroy, by ejection or collision with the central star, any terrestrial planets present in the system (Veras &amp; Armitage 2006; Raymond et al. 2010, 2011; Carrera et al.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0017291aa83cbba8\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"August 30, 2019\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b96c018c381a8b97\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExoplanet orbital and physical parameters - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b96c018c381a8b97\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exoplanet_orbital_and_physical_parameters\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd6c3ef6d3e2debb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n(<strong>As of July 2010, 55% of such exoplanets have eccentricities greater than 0.2, whereas 17% have eccentricities greater than 0.5</strong>.) Moderate to high eccentricities (e&gt;0.2) of giant planets are not an observational selection effect, because a planet can be detected about equally well regardless ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"bd6c3ef6d3e2debb\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"1 month ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6c8b156b0457c77c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMethods of detecting exoplanets - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6c8b156b0457c77c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Methods_of_detecting_exoplanets\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2b17bfb8fb1b56af\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMost confirmed extrasolar planets have been found using space-based telescopes (as of 01/2015). Many of the detection methods can work more effectively with space-based telescopes that avoid atmospheric haze and turbulence. COROT (2007-2012) and Kepler were space missions dedicated to searching for extrasolar planets using transits. COROT discovered about 30 new exoplanets. Kepler (2009-2013) and K2 (2013- ) have discovered over 2000 verified exoplanets.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2b17bfb8fb1b56af\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 22, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d13b003ab1b0d365\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRadial Velocity Planet Resources in the Exoplanet Archive\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d13b003ab1b0d365\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/docs/rv.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7ff9d8d815fdd85f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFind the Detected by Radial Velocity Variations and enter 1 in the filter box to recover the full list of RV-detected planets. The count of RV-discovered planets is available on the Exoplanet and Candidate Statistics page.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7ff9d8d815fdd85f\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 27, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0be1bbcb87f16412\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\norbital motion - Calculating the eccentricity of an exoplanet - Physics Stack Exchange\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0be1bbcb87f16412\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://physics.stackexchange.com/questions/160893/calculating-the-eccentricity-of-an-exoplanet\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d236dfe15f0a77ed\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHowever, there is another way to calculate the eccentricity: ... where $r_a$ is the radius of the apoapsis and $r_p$ the radius of the periaosis. These values are not known using only the measurements, but I believe it should be possible to calculate them by taking the integral of the sine function (radial velocity vs.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d236dfe15f0a77ed\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"physics.stackexchange.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c951dd6e06cb8c06\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDifferent Planetary Eccentricity-Period (PEP) Distributions of Small- and Giant-Planets\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c951dd6e06cb8c06\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/html/2406.09337\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"74e47387a12cab24\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe used the database of 1040104010401040 short-perio