, but there remain some major puzzles.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"03382f1840cca5e6\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"September 26, 2016\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.pnas.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "exoplanet \"eccentricity\" 0.4 0.5 0.6 radial velocity discovered 2010 2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 2016 2017 2018 2019", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 1325, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d1c4891255592128\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEvidence for a Peak at ∼0.3 in the Eccentricity Distribution of Typical Super-Jovian Exoplanets\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d1c4891255592128\">>>", "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2601.18877", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"989444477c0943d5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nOver the past 30 years of exoplanet science, this fact has enabled us to shed light on how planets form and evolve. <strong>The overall eccentricity distribution of exoplanets discovered with the radial velocity (RV) method is consistent with a beta distribution peaking at e=0</strong> (Kipping, 2013).\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"989444477c0943d5\">>>", "published": "January 23, 2026", "siteName": "arxiv.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3ebb131b1de03931\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRV-exoplanet eccentricities: Good, Beta, and Best | Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society | Oxford Academic\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3ebb131b1de03931\">>>", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/539/2/727/8098228", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"aa658921e8333834\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIn this paper, we investigate how the RV discoveries of the past |$\\sim 11$| yr affect the eccentricity distribution of RV-discovered exoplanets. Whilst most exoplanets have been discovered with the transit 