{"type": "message", "id": "42dc1136", "parentId": "7e755b2f", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:37:02.410Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:25", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"NASA Exoplanet Archive radial velocity planets eccentricity high 0.4 0.5 mass 5 Earth\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1080,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5fb147b1cc3853b9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRadial Velocity Planet Resources in the Exoplanet Archive\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5fb147b1cc3853b9\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/docs/rv.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fd3a29b2460b9f5d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe motion is detected using high-resolution spectroscopy to measure tiny Doppler shifts in the stellar spectrum toward the blue or red as the star changes its velocity along the line of sight, yielding information on the mass of the planet and its orbital period and eccentricity. Reviews of the method can be found in Radial Velocity by Lovis &amp; Fischer. Planets discovered via the transit method which meet the Archive&#x27;s exoplanet criteria are included in the Planetary Systems Table.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fd3a29b2460b9f5d\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 27, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d825033f5c769e8e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nNASA Exoplanet Archive - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d825033f5c769e8e\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exoplanet_Archive\",\n      \"d