that HD 217786 b ... MJup.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e1522341ae7153e0\">>>", "siteName": "www.raa-journal.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2426de7e74ccd9e7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nList of exoplanets detected by radial velocity - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2426de7e74ccd9e7\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_exoplanets_detected_by_radial_velocity", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"45dc0ef363e9dbfb\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe following is a list of 456 extrasolar planets that were only detected by radial velocity method –– 31 confirmed and 323 candidates, sorted by orbital periods. Since none of these planets are transiting or directly observed, they do not have measured radii and generally their masses ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"45dc0ef363e9dbfb\">>>", "published": "December 3, 2024", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c969b38a31b14b4a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDetermining the true mass of radial-velocity exoplanets with Gaia - Nine planet candidates in the brown dwarf or stellar regime and 27 confirmed planets | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c969b38a31b14b4a\">>>", "url": "https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2021/01/aa39168-20/aa39168-20.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8159555d4d94f6b6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n2011) or the NASA exoplanet archive (Akeson et al. 2013), <strong>have been detected as a consequence of radial-velocity (RV) variations of their host star</strong>. If the minimum mass m sin i, where i is a common symbolic notation for “orbital inclination”, is located below the planet/brown dwarf (BD) ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8159555d4d94f6b6\">>>", "published": "December 21, 2020", "siteName": "www.aanda.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d806c5440b36fab2\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe NASA Exoplanet Archive: Data and Tools for Exoplanet Research - ADS\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d806c5440b36fab2\">>>", "url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2013PASP..125..989A/abstract", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9020091d6329c310\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe describe the contents and functionality of the NASA Exoplanet Archive, a database and toolset funded by NASA to support astronomers in the exoplanet community. The current content of the database includes interactive tables containing properties of all published exoplanets, Kepler planet candidates, threshold-crossing events, data validation reports and target stellar parameters, light curves from the Kepler and CoRoT missions and from several ground-based surveys, and spectra and radial velocity measurements from the literature.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9020091d6329c310\">>>", "siteName": "ui.adsabs.harvard.edu"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c6dee59c5a493594\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRV-exoplanet eccentricities: Good, Beta, and Best | Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society | Oxford Academic\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c6dee59c5a493594\">>>", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/539/2/727/8098228", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"20c1b8c0f0e3e391\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSection 7 discusses the results; compares the two trialled methods; establishes the need for generating bespoke eccentricity priors on a case-by-case basis; tests how parameter inference is altered by changing the prior used; and considers some of the biases that may be present in an RV planet sample. We conclude our findings in Section 8. There are now |$\\sim 1100$| RV-detected exoplanets, of which 917 could be used for eccentricity analysis after performing the same cuts as in K13. The original catalogue used in 2013 is no longer updated, so we have elected to use the comprehensive and up to date NASA Exoplanet Archive (Akeson et al.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"20c1b8c0f0e3e391\">>>", "published": "April 12, 2025", "siteName": "academic.oup.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"926e0903d585ff26\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nnasa exoplanet archive: Topics by Science.gov\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"926e0903d585ff26\">>>", "url": "https://www.science.gov/topicpages/n/nasa+exoplanet+archive", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c8174793012ca0b3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe will deliver a catalog of detected exoplanetsâ the principal legacy of this campaignâreleased for follow-up by the astronomical community within 18 months of observation, as well as searchable archive of fully reduced images and detection limits for all stars surveyed. For each detected planet, we will produce estimated effective temperatures, luminosities, and semi-major axes: for a subset, high-SNR fiducial spectra, orbital eccentricities, and dynamical characterization through polarimetric imaging of attendant debris disks.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c8174793012ca0b3\">>>", "siteName": "www.science.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f2bc4046ec1a6905\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nExoplanets Catalogue Analysis: The Distribution of Exoplanets at FGK Stars by Mass and Orbital Period Accounting for the Observational Selection in the Radial Velocity Method | MDPI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f2bc4046ec1a6905\">>>", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4433/14/2/353", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"44288244213ead5a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIf this fraction of the orbit is small, the Kepler curve degenerates into a linear or quadratic drift of the star’s radial velocity, which indicates the presence of long-periodic bodies in the system, but does not allow their period and mass to be determined. For example, planets with P/T &gt; 2.5 (HD 221420 b, Pr0211 c, HAT-P-17 c, HR 5183 b, HD 190984 b, and HD 133131 B b) are in highly eccentric orbits.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"44288244213ead5a\">>>", "published": "February 10, 2023", "siteName": "www.mdpi.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9ecc9323989d2bd5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFig. 1.— Examples of pre-generated plots from the Exoplanet Archive....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9ecc9323989d2bd5\">>>", "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Examples-of-pre-generated-plots-from-the-Exoplanet-Archive-Right-Histogram-of-the_fig1_248394956", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a9f31e18edb21bca\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n... Distributions of period-mass (top) and period-eccentricity (bottom). Blue circles represent a selected subset of known exoplanets from the NASA Exoplanet Archive (R. Akeson et al. 2013) with measured orbital inclinations, allowing determination of true masses. Yellow circles indicate the planets studied in this work. ... Detection of Four Cold Jupiters through Combined Analyses of Radial Velocity and Astrometry Data*\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a9f31e18edb21bca\">>>", "siteName": "www.researchgate.net"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775299022408}}
{"type": "message", "id": "9e734f5f", "parentId": "42dc1136", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:37:42.881Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Based on my research, I found that HD 20794 d was discovered in 2011 using the radial velocity method at La Silla Observatory in Chile. The planet has a mass of about 4.8 Earth masses and an orbital period of about 90 days.\n\nHowever, I need to find the exoplanet with the largest eccentricity. Let me search for more specific information about exoplanets with high eccentricity."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "functions.web_search:26", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"query": "HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 0.5 0.6 high eccentric orbit", "count": 10}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "kimi-k2.5", "usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 0, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775299022411, "responseId": "chatcmpl-5e0632a4-f529-9555-8b29-7163e31cac52"}}
{"type": "message", "id": "e6c77170", "parentId": "9e734f5f", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:37:43.840Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:26", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 0.5 0.6 high eccentric orbit\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 955,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"01c86ae1fa652187\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d: A Promising Super-Earth in the Habitable Zone | by ByteBridge | Medium\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"01c86ae1fa652187\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://bytebridge.medium.com/hd-20794-d-a-promising-super-earth-in-the-habitable-zone-6c16836fc149\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"73865f91ada9d045\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDistance from Star: HD 20794 d orbits its star at a distance of 0.3499 AU (astronomical units), which is closer to its star than Earth is to the Sun (1 AU). Eccentricity: <strong>The planet has an eccentricity of 0.4, indicating an elongated orbit</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"73865f91ada9d045\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 31, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"bytebridge.medium.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d3d947613926cf72\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n82 G. Eridani d - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d3d947613926cf72\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82_G._Eridani_d\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"34144f2d7ab22cda\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe spectral lines of the host star are observed to become redder (redshift) in half of the planet&#x27;s orbit, when the host star is being pulled against Earth, and later to become bluer (blueshift) in the other half, when the host star is being pulled towards Earth. 82 G. Eridani d pulls its host star at a speed of 60 centimeters per second. 82 G. Eridani, also known as HD 20794 and e Eridani, is a nearby star visible to the unaided eye, as a faint point of light of apparent magnitude 4.25, in the constellation Eridanus.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"34144f2d7ab22cda\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"2 weeks ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a1e8b7d0cd80b29c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n82 G. Eridani - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a1e8b7d0cd80b29c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82_G._Eridani\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"410bedbd48c05842\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAnother 2023 study also only confirmed b &amp; d out of the previous planet candidates (referring to them as b &amp; c), but also detected a potential third planet farther from the star than any of the previous candidates, on an eccentric orbit partially within the habitable zone. A 2024 study confirmed this habitable zone planet. As of 2026, 82 G. Eridani is the nearest G-type main-sequence star that host confirmed exoplanets, while the closer Rigil Kentaurus, Tau Ceti and Achird have exoplanet candidates. On October 15, 2024, researchers at Oxford University announced that they had confirmed HD 20794 d, orbiting in the habitable zone of its star.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"410bedbd48c05842\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 12, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"47732e111501e057\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExploring HD 20794 d: A Super-Earth Laboratory for Habitability\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"47732e111501e057\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.seti.org/news/exploring-hd-20794-d-a-super-earth-laboratory-for-habitability/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7b27cf4dddc449a2\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nNari added, &quot;The flux of light from the star changes by a factor of seven throughout the orbit, making it a natural laboratory for studying planetary habitability.&quot; HD 20794 d does not transit its star, making atmospheric characterization challenging. However, future advancements in adaptive optics and next-generation telescopes could provide new insights despite that challenge. HD 20794 d is a compelling target for astronomers studying exoplanet habitability. Its eccentric orbit and proximity to Earth make it a valuable laboratory for understanding the conditions that might support life beyond our solar system.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7b27cf4dddc449a2\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"June 17, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.seti.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5860ed3491b9c387\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d - NASA Science\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5860ed3491b9c387\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanet-catalog/hd-20794-d/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"df6ba53ca160606e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d is a super Earth exoplanet that <strong>orbits a G-type star</strong>. Its mass is 4.8 Earths, it takes 90.3 days to complete one orbit of its star, and is 0.3499 AU from its star.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"df6ba53ca160606e\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 13, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"science.nasa.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"50acee6ae9a20b91\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nNewly discovered super-Earth orbits in and out of its star's habitable zone. Could life survive its extreme climate? | Space\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"50acee6ae9a20b91\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.space.com/the-universe/exoplanets/newly-discovered-super-earth-orbits-in-and-out-of-its-stars-habitable-zone-could-life-survive-its-extreme-climate\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f53b9bf07c499115\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nJohannes Kepler taught us that no planetary orbit is perfectly circular, most adhere to an orbit that is pretty close to being a circle. Some worlds, however, have more elongated orbits. The degree of elongation, known as eccentricity, is measured on a scale of 0 (for a perfect circle) to 1 (a hyperbola). ... Mercury&#x27;s is 0.206. HD 20794d&#x27;s orbit is more elongated than any planet in our\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f53b9bf07c499115\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 28, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.space.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f454d2a8156fbdde\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f454d2a8156fbdde\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"http://www.exoplanetkyoto.org/exohtml/HD_20794_d.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"39c468f66c4f3864\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDisplay orbital revolution of HD 20794 d around the hoststar HD 20794\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"39c468f66c4f3864\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.exoplanetkyoto.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"46908bb14ed1b42d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExoplanet HD 20794 d in the habitable zone | BBC Sky at Night Magazine\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"46908bb14ed1b42d\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.skyatnightmagazine.com/news/hd-20794-d\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"255ee8bb6652ee11\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n&quot;HD 20794, around which HD 20794 d orbits, is not an ordinary star,&quot; says Xavier Dumusque, Senior Lecturer and researcher in the Department of Astronomy at the UNIGE and co-author of the study. &quot;Its luminosity and proximity makes it an ideal candidate for future telescopes whose mission will be to observe the atmospheres of exoplanets directly.&quot; The planet itself lies in an eccentric orbit, which means its journey around the host star is not perfectly circular.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"255ee8bb6652ee11\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 29, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.skyatnightmagazine.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b471754e5587bf73\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRevisiting the multi-planetary system of the nearby star HD 20794 - Confirmation of a low-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby G-dwarf | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b471754e5587bf73\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/abs/2025/01/aa51769-24/aa51769-24.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fe1322a26e1bebee\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe confirm the presence of three planets, with periods of 18.3142 ± 0.0022 d, 89.68 ± 0.10 d, and 647.6−2.7+2.5 d, along with masses of 2.15 ± 0.17 M⊕, 2.98 ± 0.29 M⊕, and 5.82 ± 0.57 M⊕ respectively. <strong>For the outer planet, we find an eccentricity of 0.45−0.11+0.10</strong>, whereas the ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fe1322a26e1bebee\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 28, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c83ad93c4b753c87\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSuper-Earth's Long Orbit Makes It Only Occasionally Habitable | Extremetech\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c83ad93c4b753c87\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.extremetech.com/science/super-earths-long-orbit-makes-it-only-occasionally-habitable\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"29fdeebe505e8b79\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBut HD 20794d doesn&#x27;t quite circle after all, because its orbit is an oval. This isn&#x27;t unusual—most planets&#x27; orbits are sort of egg-shaped, or &quot;eccentric&quot;—but <strong>HD 20794d&#x27;s eccentricity is particularly elongated</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"29fdeebe505e8b79\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 31, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.extremetech.com\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 0.5 0.6 high eccentric orbit", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 955, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"01c86ae1fa652187\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHD 20794 d: A Promising Super-Earth in the Habitable Zone | by ByteBridge | Medium\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"01c86ae1fa652187\">>>", "url": "https://bytebridge.medium.com/hd-20794-d-a-promising-super-earth-in-the-habitable-zone-6c16836fc149", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"73865f91ada9d045\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDistance from Star: HD 20794 d orbits its star at a distance of 0.3499 AU (astronomical units), which is closer to its star than Earth is to the Sun (1 AU). Eccentricity: <strong>The planet has an eccentricity of 0.4, indicating an elongated orbit</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"73865f91ada9d045\">>>", "published": "January 31, 2025", "siteName": "bytebridge.medium.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d3d947613926cf72\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n82 G. Eridani d - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d3d947613926cf72\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82_G._Eridani_d", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"34144f2d7ab22cda\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe spectral lines of the host star are observed to become redder (redshift) in half of the planet&#x27;s orbit, when the host star is being pulled against Earth, and later to become bluer (blueshift) in the other half, when the host star is being pulled towards Earth. 82 G. Eridani d pulls its host star at a speed of 60 centimeters per second. 82 G. Eridani, also known as HD 20794 and e Eridani, is a nearby star visible to the unaided eye, as a faint point of light of apparent magnitude 4.25, in the constellation Eridanus.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"34144f2d7ab22cda\">>>", "published": "2 weeks ago", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a1e8b7d0cd80b29c\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n82 G. Eridani - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a1e8b7d0cd80b29c\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82_G._Eridani", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"410bedbd48c05842\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nAnother 2023 study also only confirmed b &amp; d out of the previous planet candidates (referring to them as b &amp; c), but also detected a potential third planet farther from the star than any of the previous candidates, on an eccentric orbit partially within the habitable zone. A 2024 study confirmed this habitable zone planet. As of 2026, 82 G. Eridani is the nearest G-type main-sequence star that host confirmed exoplanets, while the closer Rigil Kentaurus, Tau Ceti and Achird have exoplanet candidates. On October 15, 2024, researchers at Oxford University announced that they had confirmed HD 20794 d, orbiting in the habitable zone of its star.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"410bedbd48c05842\">>>", "published": "February 12, 2026", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"47732e111501e057\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nExploring HD 20794 d: A Super-Earth Laboratory for Habitability\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"47732e111501e057\">>>", "url": "https://www.seti.org/news/exploring-hd-20794-d-a-super-earth-laboratory-for-habitability/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7b27cf4dddc449a2\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nNari added, &quot;The flux of light from the star changes by a factor of seven throughout the orbit, making it a natural laboratory for studying planetary habitability.&quot; HD 20794 d does not transit its star, making atmospheric characterization challenging. However, future advancements in adaptive optics and next-generation telescopes could provide new insights despite that challenge. HD 20794 d is a compelling target for astronomers studying exoplanet habitability. Its eccentric orbit and proximity to Earth make it a valuable laboratory for understanding the conditions that might support life beyond our solar system.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7b27cf4dddc449a2\">>>", "published": "June 17, 2025", "siteName": "www.seti.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5860ed3491b9c387\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHD 20794 d - NASA Science\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5860ed3491b9c387\">>>", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanet-catalog/hd-20794-d/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"df6ba53ca160606e\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHD 20794 d is a super Earth exoplanet that <strong>orbits a G-type star</strong>. Its mass is 4.8 Earths, it takes 90.3 days to complete one orbit of its star, and is 0.3499 AU from its star.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"df6ba53ca160606e\">>>", "published": "February 13, 2026", "siteName": "science.nasa.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"50acee6ae9a20b91\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nNewly discovered super-Earth orbits in and out of its star's habitable zone. Could life survive its extreme climate? | Space\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"50acee6ae9a20b91\">>>", "url": "https://www.space.com/the-universe/exoplanets/newly-discovered-super-earth-orbits-in-and-out-of-its-stars-habitable-zone-could-life-survive-its-extreme-climate", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f53b9bf07c499115\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nJohannes Kepler taught us that no planetary orbit is perfectly circular, most adhere to an orbit that is pretty close to being a circle. Some worlds, however, have more elongated orbits. The degree of elongation, known as eccentricity, is measured on a scale of 0 (for a perfect circle) to 1 (a hyperbola). ... Mercury&#x27;s is 0.206. HD 20794d&#x27;s orbit is more elongated than any planet in our\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f53b9bf07c499115\">>>", "published": "January 28, 2025", "siteName": "www.space.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f454d2a8156fbdde\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHD 20794 d\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f454d2a8156fbdde\">>>", "url": "http://www.exoplanetkyoto.org/exohtml/HD_20794_d.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"39c468f66c4f3864\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDisplay orbital revolution of HD 20794 d around the hoststar HD 20794\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"39c468f66c4f3864\">>>", "siteName": "www.exoplanetkyoto.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"46908bb14ed1b42d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nExoplanet HD 20794 d in the habitable zone | BBC Sky at Night Magazine\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"46908bb14ed1b42d\">>>", "url": "https://www.skyatnightmagazine.com/news/hd-20794-d", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"255ee8bb6652ee11\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n&quot;HD 20794, around which HD 20794 d orbits, is not an ordinary star,&quot; says Xavier Dumusque, Senior Lecturer and researcher in the Department of Astronomy at the UNIGE and co-author of the study. &quot;Its luminosity and proximity makes it an ideal candidate for future telescopes whose mission will be to observe the atmospheres of exoplanets directly.&quot; The planet itself lies in an eccentric orbit, which means its journey around the host star is not perfectly circular.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"255ee8bb6652ee11\">>>", "published": "January 29, 2025", "siteName": "www.skyatnightmagazine.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b471754e5587bf73\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRevisiting the multi-planetary system of the nearby star HD 20794 - Confirmation of a low-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby G-dwarf | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b471754e5587bf73\">>>", "url": "https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/abs/2025/01/aa51769-24/aa51769-24.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fe1322a26e1bebee\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe confirm the presence of three planets, with periods of 18.3142 ± 0.0022 d, 89.68 ± 0.10 d, and 647.6−2.7+2.5 d, along with masses of 2.15 ± 0.17 M⊕, 2.98 ± 0.29 M⊕, and 5.82 ± 0.57 M⊕ respectively. <strong>For the outer planet, we find an eccentricity of 0.45−0.11+0.10</strong>, whereas the ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fe1322a26e1bebee\">>>", "published": "January 28, 2025", "siteName": "www.aanda.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c83ad93c4b753c87\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSuper-Earth's Long Orbit Makes It Only Occasionally Habitable | Extremetech\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c83ad93c4b753c87\">>>", "url": "https://www.extremetech.com/science/super-earths-long-orbit-makes-it-only-occasionally-habitable", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"29fdeebe505e8b79\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nBut HD 20794d doesn&#x27;t quite circle after all, because its orbit is an oval. This isn&#x27;t unusual—most planets&#x27; orbits are sort of egg-shaped, or &quot;eccentric&quot;—but <strong>HD 20794d&#x27;s eccentricity is particularly elongated</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"29fdeebe505e8b79\">>>", "published": "January 31, 2025", "siteName": "www.extremetech.com"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775299063837}}
{"type": "message", "id": "e6142265", "parentId": "e6c77170", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:37:59.513Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I found that HD 20794 d has an eccentricity of 0.4 according to one source, and the 2025 paper mentions an outer planet with eccentricity 0.45. However, I need to check if the original HD 20794 d discovered in 2011 has an eccentricity of 0.4 or if this is a different planet.\n\nLet me search for more information about the original 2011 discovery."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "functions.web_search:27", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"query": "Pepe 2011 HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 radial velocity HARPS", "count": 10}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "kimi-k2.5", "usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 0, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775299063841, "responseId": "chatcmpl-3675c069-8c45-963d-8afe-56d1f6be47e8"}}
{"type": "message", "id": "97476cf8", "parentId": "e6142265", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:38:00.430Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:27", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"Pepe 2011 HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 radial velocity HARPS\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 911,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b4e280abbc8e3d90\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nA&A 534, A58 (2011) DOI: 10.1051/0004-6361/201117055 c⃝ESO 2011 Astronomy &\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b4e280abbc8e3d90\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/pdf/2011/10/aa17055-11.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c7a904acce8ea4b0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nF. Pepe et al.: The HARPS search for Earth-like planets in the habitable zone. I. Fig. 22. Top: residuals of the radial velocities of HD 20794 to the three-\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c7a904acce8ea4b0\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6bb7d6c5e0194abc\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEvidence for at least three planet candidates orbiting HD 20794 | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6bb7d6c5e0194abc\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2017/09/aa30406-17/aa30406-17.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1fb437b053e7d179\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn this work, we apply this noise model framework to the HARPS RV measurements of HD 20794. HD 20794 is a <strong>high-velocity and metal-deficient G8 star</strong> with a mass of ... 0.018 M⊙ (Ramírez 