{"type": "message", "id": "119442d1", "parentId": "97476cf8", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:38:16.921Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Based on my research, I found that HD 20794 d was discovered in 2011 by Pepe et al. using the radial velocity method at La Silla Observatory in Chile. The planet has a mass of about 4.8 Earth masses and an orbital period of about 90 days. The eccentricity is mentioned as 0.4 in some sources.\n\nHowever, I need to verify if this is the exoplanet with the largest eccentricity meeting the criteria. Let me search for more exoplanets with high eccentricity discovered by radial velocity before 2020."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "functions.web_search:28", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"query": "exoplanets radial velocity eccentricity 0.5 0.6 0.7 discovered before 2020 mass 5 Earth", "count": 10}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "kimi-k2.5", "usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 0, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775299080432, "responseId": "chatcmpl-579ed025-f638-9d5c-84f0-f765644b348c"}}
{"type": "message", "id": "166ce40a", "parentId": "119442d1", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:38:17.995Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:28", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"exoplanets radial velocity eccentricity 0.5 0.6 0.7 discovered before 2020 mass 5 Earth\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 1071,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c39d876fd2801eed\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nList of exoplanets detected by radial velocity - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c39d876fd2801eed\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_exoplanets_detected_by_radial_velocity\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e8c9fad7a716547\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe following is a list of 456 extrasolar planets that were only detected by radial velocity method –– <strong>31 confirmed and 323 candidates</strong>, sorted by orbital periods. Since none of these planets are transiting or directly observed, they do not have measured radii and generally their masses ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7e8c9fad7a716547\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 3, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8bd0939e655b3b73\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExoplanets Catalogue Analysis: The Distribution of Exoplanets at FGK Stars by Mass and Orbital Period Accounting for the Observational Selection in the Radial Velocity Method | MDPI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8bd0939e655b3b73\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4433/14/2/353\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a343c16ce464f2c2\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIf this fraction of the orbit is small, the Kepler curve degenerates into a linear or quadratic drift of the star’s radial velocity, which indicates the presence of long-periodic bodies in the system, but does not allow their period and mass to be determined. For example, planets with P/T &gt; 2.5 (HD 221420 b, Pr0211 c, HAT-P-17 c, HR 5183 b, HD 190984 b, and HD 133131 B b) are in highly eccentric orbits.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a343c16ce464f2c2\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 10, 2023\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.mdpi.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb407ef57fc26c98\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nInstituto de Astrofísica e Ciências do Espaço\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb407ef57fc26c98\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.iastro.pt/research/paperDetails.html?ID=3040\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb9b39c53ba3be50\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFurther analysis of these RVs reveals the presence of an additional planet, <strong>HD 35843 b</strong>, with a period of 9.90 days and a minimum mass of 5.84 ± 0.84 M⊕. For HD 35843 c, a joint photometric and spectroscopic analysis yields a radius of 2.54 ± 0.08 R⊕, a mass of 11.32 ± 1.60 M⊕, and ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb9b39c53ba3be50\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.iastro.pt\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ad2c1ceae8ff0bf6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRV-exoplanet eccentricities: Good, Beta, and Best | Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society | Oxford Academic\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ad2c1ceae8ff0bf6\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/539/2/727/8098228\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"34ceafd77e9f28dd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe examine the eccentricity distribution(s) of radial velocity (RV) detected exoplanets. Previously, the eccentricity distribution was found to be described well by a Beta distribution with shape parameters |$a=0.867\\\\,\\\\mathrm{ and}\\\\, b=3.03$|⁠. Increasing the sample size by a factor of 2.25, we find that the cumulative distribution function regression method now prefers a mixture model of Rayleigh + Exponential distributions over the Beta distribution, with an increase in Bayesian evidence of |$\\\\Delta \\\\ln {Z}\\\\sim 77$| (⁠|$12.6\\\\, \\\\sigma$|⁠).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"34ceafd77e9f28dd\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 12, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"academic.oup.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"42756d067f9ec0d1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGiant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT ‘EM) Survey. II. Discovery of a Failed Hot Jupiter on a 2.7 Yr, Highly Eccentric Orbit* | Request PDF\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"42756d067f9ec0d1\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354732066_Giant_Outer_Transiting_Exoplanet_Mass_GOT_'EM_Survey_II_Discovery_of_a_Failed_Hot_Jupiter_on_a_27_Yr_Highly_Eccentric_Orbit\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5336712cb1f3bcbe\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThis supports the hypothesis that dynamical interactions with wide stellar companions plays an important role in the formation of highly eccentric exoplanets. ... Paul A. Dalba · Heather A. Knutson ... The observed correlation between outer giant planets and inner super-Earths is emerging as an important constraint on planet formation theories. In this study we focus on Kepler-167, which is currently the only system known to contain both inner transiting super-Earths and a confirmed outer transiting gas giant companion beyond 1 au. Using long term radial velocity monitoring, we measure the mass of the gas giant Kepler-167e (P = 1071 days) to be 1.01^(+0.16)_(−0.15) M_J, thus confirming it as a Jupiter analog.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5336712cb1f3bcbe\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"October 1, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.researchgate.net\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"59783f3c703dd2d7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDetection and characterisation of a 106-day transiting Jupiter: TOI-2449 b/NGTS-36 b | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"59783f3c703dd2d7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/11/aa55168-25/aa55168-25.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c5f217b50587a66d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe report the discovery and characterisation of <strong>TOI-2449 b / NGTS-36 b</strong>, a 106-day warm giant exoplanet orbiting a G-type star. This planet was first identified as a single transit event in the TESS data. Ground-based photometric follow-up with NGTS and radial velocity follow-up solved the orbital ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c5f217b50587a66d\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"November 24, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"12754d9c1ca7c211\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRadial Velocity Planet Resources in the Exoplanet Archive\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"12754d9c1ca7c211\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/docs/rv.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8446c657f02a9566\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe motion is detected using high-resolution spectroscopy to measure tiny Doppler shifts in the stellar spectrum toward the blue or red as the star changes its velocity along the line of sight, yielding information on the mass of the planet and its orbital period and eccentricity. Reviews of the method can be found in Radial Velocity by Lovis &amp; Fischer. Planets discovered via the transit method which meet the Archive&#x27;s exoplanet criteria are included in the Planetary Systems Table.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8446c657f02a9566\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"April 27, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"af542cf120279b06\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDiscoveries of exoplanets - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"af542cf120279b06\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discoveries_of_exoplanets\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6207265329c67e14\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAs of 26 February 2026, there are 6,256 confirmed exoplanets in 4,664 planetary systems, with 1,050 systems having more than one planet. This is a list of the most notable discoveries. Tadmor: The radial velocity variations of the star Errai were announced in 1989, consistent with a planet in a 2.5-year orbit.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6207265329c67e14\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"2 weeks ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f86381af37f58e58\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGJ 2126 b: A highly eccentric Jovian exoplanet | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f86381af37f58e58\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/02/aa52563-24/aa52563-24.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0ba0946e820c6f52\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe report the discovery of <strong>GJ 2126 b</strong>, a highly eccentric (e = 0.85) Jupiter-like planet orbiting its host star every 272.7 days. The planet was detected and characterized using 112 radial velocity (RV) measurements from HARPS (High Accuracy Radial velocity Planet Searcher), provided by HARPS-RVBank.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0ba0946e820c6f52\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 14, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3b5c12d72db4ffa6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRadial velocity - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3b5c12d72db4ffa6\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radial_velocity\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"96ec5e00a1014651\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIt has been suggested that planets with high eccentricities calculated by this method may in fact be two-planet systems of circular or near-circular resonant orbit. The radial velocity method to detect exoplanets is based on the detection of variations in the velocity of the central star, due to the changing direction of the gravitational pull from an (unseen) exoplanet as it orbits the star.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"96ec5e00a1014651\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"1 week ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "exoplanets radial velocity eccentricity 0.5 0.6 0.7 discovered before 2020 mass 5 Earth", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 1071, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c39d876fd2801eed\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nList of exoplanets detected by radial velocity - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c39d876fd2801eed\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_exoplanets_detected_by_radial_velocity", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7e8c9fad7a716547\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe following is a list of 456 extrasolar planets that were only detected by radial velocity method –– <strong>31 confirmed and 323 candidates</strong>, sorted by orbital periods. Since none of these planets are transiting or directly observed, they do not have measured radii and generally their masses ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7e8c9fad7a716547\">>>", "published": "December 3, 2024", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8bd0939e655b3b73\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nExoplanets Catalogue Analysis: The Distribution of Exoplanets at FGK Stars by Mass and Orbital Period Accounting for the Observational Selection in the Radial Velocity Method | MDPI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8bd0939e655b3b73\">>>", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4433/14/2/353", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a343c16ce464f2c2\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIf this fraction of the orbit is small, the Kepler curve degenerates into a linear or quadratic drift of the star’s radial velocity, which indicates the presence of long-periodic bodies in the system, but does not allow their period and mass to be determined. For example, planets with P/T &gt; 2.5 (HD 221420 b, Pr0211 c, HAT-P-17 c, HR 5183 b, HD 190984 b, and HD 133131 B b) are in highly eccentric orbits.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a343c16ce464f2c2\">>>", "published": "February 10, 2023", "siteName": "www.mdpi.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb407ef57fc26c98\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nInstituto de Astrofísica e Ciências do Espaço\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb407ef57fc26c98\">>>", "url": "https://www.iastro.pt/research/paperDetails.html?ID=3040", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb9b39c53ba3be50\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFurther analysis of these RVs reveals the presence of an additional planet, <strong>HD 35843 b</strong>, with a period of 9.90 days and a minimum mass of 5.84 ± 0.84 M⊕. For HD 35843 c, a joint photometric and spectroscopic analysis yields a radius of 2.54 ± 0.08 R⊕, a mass of 11.32 ± 1.60 M⊕, and ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb9b39c53ba3be50\">>>", "siteName": "www.iastro.pt"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ad2c1ceae8ff0bf6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRV-exoplanet eccentricities: Good, Beta, and Best | Monthly Notices of the Royal Astronomical Society | Oxford Academic\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ad2c1ceae8ff0bf6\">>>", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/539/2/727/8098228", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"34ceafd77e9f28dd\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe examine the eccentricity distribution(s) of radial velocity (RV) detected exoplanets. Previously, the eccentricity distribution was found to be described well by a Beta distribution with shape parameters |$a=0.867\\,\\mathrm{ and}\\, b=3.03$|⁠. Increasing the sample size by a factor of 2.25, we find that the cumulative distribution function regression method now prefers a mixture model of Rayleigh + Exponential distributions over the Beta distribution, with an increase in Bayesian evidence of |$\\Delta \\ln {Z}\\sim 77$| (⁠|$12.6\\, \\sigma$|⁠).\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"34ceafd77e9f28dd\">>>", "published": "April 12, 2025", "siteName": "academic.oup.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"42756d067f9ec0d1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nGiant Outer Transiting Exoplanet Mass (GOT ‘EM) Survey. II. Discovery of a Failed Hot Jupiter on a 2.7 Yr, Highly Eccentric Orbit* | Request PDF\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"42756d067f9ec0d1\">>>", "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/354732066_Giant_Outer_Transiting_Exoplanet_Mass_GOT_'EM_Survey_II_Discovery_of_a_Failed_Hot_Jupiter_on_a_27_Yr_Highly_Eccentric_Orbit", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5336712cb1f3bcbe\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThis supports the hypothesis that dynamical interactions with wide stellar companions plays an important role in the formation of highly eccentric exoplanets. ... Paul A. Dalba · Heather A. Knutson ... The observed correlation between outer giant planets and inner super-Earths is emerging as an important constraint on planet formation theories. In this study we focus on Kepler-167, which is currently the only system known to contain both inner transiting super-Earths and a confirmed outer transiting gas giant companion beyond 1 au. Using long term radial velocity monitoring, we measure the mass of the gas giant Kepler-167e (P = 1071 days) to be 1.01^(+0.16)_(−0.15) M_J, thus confirming it as a Jupiter analog.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5336712cb1f3bcbe\">>>", "published": "October 1, 2021", "siteName": "www.researchgate.net"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"59783f3c703dd2d7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDetection and characterisation of a 106-day transiting Jupiter: TOI-2449 b/NGTS-36 b | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"59783f3c703dd2d7\">>>", "url": "https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2025/11/aa55168-25/aa55168-25.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c5f217b50587a66d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nWe report the discovery and characterisation of <strong>TOI-2449 b / NGTS-36 b</strong>, a 106-day warm giant exoplanet orbiting a G-type star. This planet was first identified as a single transit event in the TESS data. Ground-based photometric follow-up with NGTS and radial velocity follow-up solved the orbital ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c5f217b50587a66d\">>>", "published": "November 24, 2025", "siteName": "www.aanda.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"12754d9c1ca7c211\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRadial Velocity Planet Resources in the Exoplanet Archive\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"12754d9c1ca7c211\">>>", "url": "https://exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu/docs/rv.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8446c657f02a9566\">>>\nS