t orbiting its host star every 272.7 days. The planet was detected and characterized using 112 radial velocity (RV) measurements from HARPS (High Accuracy Radial velocity Planet Searcher), provided by HARPS-RVBank.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0ba0946e820c6f52\">>>", "published": "February 14, 2025", "siteName": "www.aanda.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3b5c12d72db4ffa6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRadial velocity - Wikipedia\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3b5c12d72db4ffa6\">>>", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radial_velocity", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"96ec5e00a1014651\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIt has been suggested that planets with high eccentricities calculated by this method may in fact be two-planet systems of circular or near-circular resonant orbit. The radial velocity method to detect exoplanets is based on the detection of variations in the velocity of the central star, due to the changing direction of the gravitational pull from an (unseen) exoplanet as it orbits the star.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"96ec5e00a1014651\">>>", "published": "1 week ago", "siteName": "en.wikipedia.org"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1775299097993}}
{"type": "message", "id": "0535a797", "parentId": "166ce40a", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:38:51.978Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I found that GJ 2126 b has an eccentricity of 0.85, but it was discovered in 2025, which is after the cutoff of 2020. Let me search for more exoplanets with high eccentricity discovered before 2020."}, {"type": "toolCall", "id": "functions.web_search:29", "name": "web_search", "arguments": {"query": "HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 radial velocity 2011 discovery Chile", "count": 10}}], "api": "openai-completions", "provider": "modelstudio", "model": "kimi-k2.5", "usage": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "totalTokens": 0, "cost": {"input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0, "total": 0}}, "stopReason": "toolUse", "timestamp": 1775299097997, "responseId": "chatcmpl-8efaae8e-5a85-96bc-bdc7-378abc312eae"}}
{"type": "message", "id": "e322f0fa", "parentId": "0535a797", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T10:38:52.948Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:29", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 radial velocity 2011 discovery Chile\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 966,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"793e5023ad439e62\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d - NASA Science\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"793e5023ad439e62\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanet-catalog/hd-20794-d/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"71b5fc5544a552e9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d is a super Earth exoplanet that orbits a G-type star. Its mass is 4.8 Earths, it takes 90.3 days to complete one orbit of its star, and is 0.3499 AU from its star. <strong>Its discovery was announced in 2011.</strong>\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"71b5fc5544a552e9\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 13, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"science.nasa.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d2050d1a7ace6b9e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRevisiting the multi-planetary system of the nearby star HD 20794 - Confirmation of a low-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby G-dwarf | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d2050d1a7ace6b9e\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/component/article?access=doi&doi=10.1051/0004-6361/202451769\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fdebe1d1da9ab231\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nWe confirm the presence of three planets, with periods of 18.3142 ± 0.0022 d, 89.68 ± 0.10 d, and 647.6−2.7+2.5 d, along with masses of 2.15 ± 0.17 M⊕, 2.98 ± 0.29 M⊕, and 5.82 ± 0.57 M⊕ respectively. <strong>For the outer planet, we find an eccentricity of 0.45−0.11+0.10</strong>, whereas the ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fdebe1d1da9ab231\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 28, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0b04885216ca92dd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n82 G. Eridani d - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0b04885216ca92dd\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82_G._Eridani_d\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8135865f17aaa585\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n82 G. Eridani d (HD 20794 d) is an exoplanet, possibly rocky, orbiting the solar-type star 82 G. Eridani. It is at least five times more massive than Earth and has an orbit partially within the habitable zone of its host star. Depending on its physical characteristics, which are poorly known, ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8135865f17aaa585\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"2 weeks ago\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1d01c3c3039183ad\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHD 20794 d: A Promising Super-Earth in the Habitable Zone | by ByteBridge | Medium\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1d01c3c3039183ad\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://bytebridge.medium.com/hd-20794-d-a-promising-super-earth-in-the-habitable-zone-6c16836fc149\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f53133c1fa9e4119\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe eccentric orbit causes periods ... in 2011 using the radial velocity technique, which <strong>detects the minute gravitational influence of the planet on its host star</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f53133c1fa9e4119\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 31, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"bytebridge.medium.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ba1faf48328a5c88\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n82 G. Eridani - Wikipedia\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ba1faf48328a5c88\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/82_G._Eridani\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ee2fb42513802d30\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nData for the research was gathered at the High Accuracy Radial Velocity Planet Searcher spectrograph at La Silla Observatory in Chile. With the discovery, HD 20794 d became the <strong>12th-closest potentially habitable planet to Earth</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ee2fb42513802d30\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 12, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"en.wikipedia.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9b1f17577aad4351\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nNewly discovered super-Earth orbits in and out of its star's habitable zone. Could life survive its extreme climate? | Space\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9b1f17577aad4351\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.space.com/the-universe/exoplanets/newly-discovered-super-earth-orbits-in-and-out-of-its-stars-habitable-zone-could-life-survive-its-extreme-climate\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c9dc745303057d03\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>The origins of such a large degree of eccentricity lie in the HD 20794d&#x27;s distant past</strong>, Dumusque tells Space.com. &quot;The eccentricity of planets are a remnant of planet–planet interactions during the early days of a planetary system,&quot; he says.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c9dc745303057d03\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 28, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.space.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b58d565b7e0d1f7b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nExoplanet HD 20794 d in the habitable zone | BBC Sky at Night Magazine\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b58d565b7e0d1f7b\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.skyatnightmagazine.com/news/hd-20794-d\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8d684ad0aaa536e0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCredit: Y. Beletsky (LCO)/ESO · <strong>Dr Michael Cretignier from the University of Oxford in the UK first discovered the exoplanet candidate that would later be confirmed as HD 20794 d</strong>. He was analysing archive data recorded by the HARPS (High Accuracy ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8d684ad0aaa536e0\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 29, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.skyatnightmagazine.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3d5ff243aff69038\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nOpen Exoplanet Catalogue - HD 20794 d\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3d5ff243aff69038\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://openexoplanetcatalogue.com/planet/HD%2020794%20d/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7eed965b254a4707\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThis table lists all stars in the system HD 20794. The following plot shows the approximate sizes of the planets in this system The Solar System planets are shown as a comparison. Note that unless the radius has been determined through a transit observation, this is only an approximation (see Lissauer et al. 2011b).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7eed965b254a4707\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"openexoplanetcatalogue.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"84e5cf6dc275af83\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEvidence for at least three planet candidates orbiting HD 20794 | Astronomy & Astrophysics (A&A)\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"84e5cf6dc275af83\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2017/09/aa30406-17/aa30406-17.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"aa91b5ed1581af13\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe signal at a period of about 330 d probably correspond to a Neptune located in the habitable zone of HD 20794. The estimated orbital eccentricity of HD 20794 b, d and e is larger than 0.2. This considerable eccentricity is <strong>probably caused by instrumental noise and the fitting bias</strong>, as concluded ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"aa91b5ed1581af13\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"September 18, 2017\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.aanda.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"89cf326f3851b378\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n[2501.17092] Revisiting the multi-planetary system of the nearby star HD 20794: Confirmation of a low-mass planet in the habitable zone of a nearby G-dwarf\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"89cf326f3851b378\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17092\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2dc75208537f2339\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>For the outer planet, we find an eccentricity of 0.45 +/- 0.10</strong>, whereas the inner planets are compatible with circular orbits. The latter is likely to be a rocky planet in the habitable zone of HD 20794.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2dc75208537f2339\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 28, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"arxiv.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "HD 20794 d eccentricity 0.4 radial velocity 2011 discovery Chile", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 966, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"793e5023ad439e62\">>>\nSource: Web 